Article 14 (Scope of mountainous districts subject to purchase) (1) The mountainous districts subject to purchase under Article 13(1) of the Act shall be mountainous districts other than those designated or determined as the areas, etc. for specific uses by the heads of relevant administrative agencies under other statutes (excluding the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act and the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act). <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Aug. 4, 2006>
  (2) Where the mountainous districts located in the restricted areas for conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts are purchased under Article 13(1) of the Act, the timing, methods, and standards for determining the purchase price shall be governed, mutatis mutandis, by Articles 67(1), 68, 70, 74, 75, 75-2, 76, 77, and 78(6) through (8) of the Act on the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects. <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Dec. 7, 2010; May 9, 2022>

SECTION 3 Permission for Conversion of Mountainous Districts

Article 14-2 (Procedures for request for purchase of mountainous districts) (1) A person who intends to request purchase of a mountainous district under Article 13-2(1) of the Act shall submit a written request for purchase of a mountainous district in the form prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 7, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) When a claim for purchase is made under paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall, within 60 days from the date of the claim, notify the claimant of whether the land is subject to purchase and other relevant matters, and if the land is deemed subject to purchase, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall purchase the claimed mountainous district within 3 years from the date of such notification.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 15 (Procedures and examination of permission for conversion of mountainous districts) (1) A person who intends to obtain permission for conversion of a mountainous district, permission for modifications thereof, or file a notification of modifications under Article 14(1) of the Act shall, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, demarcate the boundaries of the area subject to permission for conversion of mountainous districts or permission for modifications thereof, or notification of modifications, and shall submit an application form, attached with documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, to the competent authorities according to following classifications: <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 11, 2015; Dec. 30, 2016; Jun. 2, 2017>
  1. In cases of seeking permission for conversion of mountainous districts under Article 14(1) of the Act:
  a. Where the area of the mountainous district subject to permission for forest conversion under Article 14(1) of the Act is 2 million square meters or more (1 million square meters or more in the case of conservation mountainous districts): The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service;
  b. Where the area of mountainous districts seeking permission for conversion of mountainous districts under Article 14(1) of the Act is 500,000 square meters or more but less than 2 million square meters (in the case of conservation mountainous districts, 30,000 square meters or more but less than 1 million square meters):
  1) In cases of mountainous districts in the State forest under the jurisdiction of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service: The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service;
  2) In cases of mountainous districts in the State forest, public forest, or private forest not under the jurisdiction of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service: City/Do Governor;
  c. Where the area of the mountainous district for which permission for conversion is sought under Article 14(1) of the Act is less than 500,000 square meters (less than 30,000 square meters in the case of conservation forest):
  1) In cases of mountainous districts in the State forest under the jurisdiction of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service: The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service;
  2) In cases of mountainous districts in the State forest, public forest, or private forest not under the jurisdiction of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service: The head of a Si/Gun/Gu;
  2. Where a person seeks permission for modifications or intends to file a notification of modifications regarding permission for conversion of mountainous districts under Article 14(1) of the Act: The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or any other person who granted the relevant permission for conversion of mountainous district under Article 14(1) of the Act;
  3. Deleted. <Jun. 2, 2017>
  (2) Upon receiving an application for permission for conversion of mountainous districts or permission for modifications thereof, or upon receiving a notification of modifications under paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or other competent authorities shall conduct an on-site inspection, including verification of boundary demarcation, of the mountainous district subject to such permission, permission for modifications, or notification of modifications, and shall examine whether the details of application are in compliance with the criteria for permission stipulated in Article 18 of the Act; provided, where a feasibility study on the conversion of mountainous districts has been conducted under Article 18-2 of the Act, the examination may be conducted without an on-site inspection. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Dec. 30, 2016>
  (3) Where, as a result of the examination under paragraph (2), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. deems it appropriate to grant permission for conversion of a mountainous district or permission for modifications thereof, or accept a notification of modifications, they shall issue a permit for the conversion of a mountainous district as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs to the applicant, or accept the notification; provided, if the applicant is required to prepay the substitute forest resource development charges under Article 19(1) of the Act or to make restoration deposit in advance under the main clause of Article 38(1) of the Act, the permit for conversion of mountainous district shall be issued or the notification be accepted only after confirming such payment or deposit. <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Jul. 27, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 30, 2016>

Article 16 (Consultation on conversion of mountainous districts) (1) Where the head of a relevant administrative agency requests consultation with the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or other competent authority regarding conversion of mountainous districts under Article 14(4) of the Act, the head shall submit (including submission by electronic document) a written request for consultations on the conversion of mountainous districts, accompanied by documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 2, 2020>
  (2) Article 15(2) shall apply mutatis mutandis to examination of requests for consultations on conversion of mountainous districts under paragraph (1).

Article 17 (Notification of conversion of mountainous districts) (1) A person who intends to file a notification for the conversion of mountainous districts or a notification of modifications under Article 15(1) of the Act shall submit a notification form for the conversion of mountainous districts, along with the documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. In such cases, a person filing the notification shall mark the boundaries of the relevant area as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 7, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) In Article 15(1)1 of the Act, "Forest public facilities and their ancillary facilities as prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the following facilities: <Added on Aug. 22, 2012; Nov. 11, 2015; Dec. 30, 2016; Oct. 30, 2018; Jun. 7, 2023>
  1. Facilities for forest management, mountain village development, and forestry testing and research, and their ancillary facilities;
  2. Arboretums, forest ecological gardens, natural recreation forests and their ancillary facilities;
  3. Forest bathing areas, healing forests, forest camping sites, forest sports facilities, forest management experience forests and their ancillary facilities according to subparagraphs 3, 5, 8, 9 and 10 of Article 2 of the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act;
  4. Forest experience centers for children, forest education centers and their ancillary facilities under Articles 12 and 13 of the Forest Education Promotion Act;
  5. Forestry welfare facilities and their ancillary facilities within a forestry welfare complex established with approval of the implementation plan under Article 35 of the Forest Welfare Promotion Act.
  (3) In Article 15(1)3 of the Act, "facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as warehouses, collection centers, and processing facilities for agricultural, forestry, and fishery products" means any of the following facilities: <Added on Jul. 27, 2007; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Dec. 30, 2016>
  1. Warehouses, collection centers, and processing facilities for agricultural, livestock, and fishery products;
  2. Agricultural machinery repair facilities and agricultural machinery warehouses;
  3. Insect rearing facilities and management facilities, including those for silkworms.

Article 18 (Scope of facilities and activities subject to notification of conversion of mountainous districts) The scope of facilities and activities subject to notification of conversion of mountainous districts under Article 15(2) of the Act, the installation areas, and installation requirements shall be as prescribed in Appendix 3.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Oct. 30, 2018]

Article 18-2 (Permission for temporary use of mountainous districts) (1) Article 15(1) through (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures and examination of permission for temporary use of a mountainous district, permission for modifications thereof, or notification of modifications under Article 15-2(1) of the Act. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012>
  (2) "Purpose prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 15-2(1) of the Act means any of the following uses: <Amended on Jan. 28, 2011; May 22, 2012; Aug. 22, 2012; Dec. 17, 2013; Aug. 12, 2014; Oct. 30, 2018; Dec. 4, 2018; Aug. 23, 2022; May 7, 2024>
  1. Installation of distribution facilities, telecommunications transmission facilities, wind power generation facilities, and wind measurement facilities;
  2. Installation of tramway facilities in accordance with the Tramway Transportation Act;
  3. Excavation of buried heritage in accordance with the Act on Protection and Inspection of Buried Heritage;
  4. Other uses similar to those specified in subparagraphs 1 through 3, as determined and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  (3) The scope of facilities and activities subject to permission for temporary use of mountainous districts, areas to be installed, and conditions and standards for installation under Article 15-2(5) of the Act shall be as specified in Appendix 3-2. <Amended on Oct. 30, 2018; Jun 2, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 7, 2010]

Article 18-3 (Notification of temporary use of mountainous districts) (1) Article 17(1) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures for notification of temporary use of a mountainous district or notification of modifications thereof under Article 15-2(4) of the Act. <Amended on Jun. 2, 2020>
  (2) In Article 15-2(4)10 of the Act, "facilities related to disaster emergency measures prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the facilities that fall under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on May 22, 2012; Aug. 22, 2012; Nov. 11, 2015; Jun. 2, 2020; Jan. 30, 2026>
  1. Facilities for the prevention and suppression of forest fires, such as fire watchtowers, firebreaks, simple wireless communication facilities, temporary water tanks, and temporary helipads;
  2. Facilities necessary for the control of forest diseases and pests under subparagraph 8c of Article 2 of the Forest Disaster Prevention Act;
  3. Facilities necessary for the culling of livestock under Article 20 of the Act on the Prevention of Contagious Animal Diseases and for the incineration and burial of livestock carcasses under Article 22 of that Act;
  4. Facilities required for emergency measures under Article 37(1) of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety.
  (3) In Article 15-2(4)11 of the Act, "a scale prescribed by Presidential Decree" means 100 square meters. <Added on Aug. 22, 2012; Dec. 30, 2016; Jun. 2, 2020>
  (4) The scope of facilities and activities subject to notification of temporary use of mountainous districts, installation areas, and installation conditions under Article 15-2(5) of the Act are as specified in Appendix 3-3. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Oct. 30, 2018; Jun. 2, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 7, 2010]

Article 18-4 (Period for temporary use of mountainous districts) (1) The period for temporary use of mountainous districts under Article 15-2(5) of the Act shall be as specified in the following subparagraphs; provided, where a person who intends to obtain a temporary use permit for mountainous district or to file a temporary use notification of mountainous district is not the owner of the mountainous district, the period of use shall not exceed the period during which the person is entitled to use or benefit from the mountainous district: <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 2, 2020>
  1. In the case of permission for temporary use of mountainous districts: The period permitted by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. by up to 10 years, taking into account the area of mountainous districts for temporary use and the principal project for temporary use, in accordance with the standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs;
  1. In the case of permission for temporary use of mountainous districts: The period permitted by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. by up to 10 years, taking into account the area of mountainous districts for temporary use and the intended project for temporary use, in accordance with the standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs;
  (2) Article 19 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the extension of the period for temporary use of mountainous districts under paragraph (1).
  (3) Notwithstanding the main clause of Article 19(2) which applies mutatis mutandis under paragraph (2), permission for the extension of the period for temporary use of mountainous districts may be granted in excess of the initial period for temporary use of mountainous districts in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 28, 2023>
  1. In case of extending the period for temporary use of a mountainous district for mining of minerals in accordance with the Mining Industry Act, where all of the following requirements are satisfied:
  a. It shall be deemed reasonable to extend the period for temporary use of mountainous districts for mining minerals under the Mining Industry Act;
  b. The initial period of temporary use of mountainous districts, any previously extended period of temporary use of mountainous districts, and the period of extension being sought shall be aggregated and shall not exceed the duration of the mining right as provided in Article 12 of the Mining Industry Act;
  2. Where it is intended to extend the period for temporary use of a mountainous district for wind power generation facilities, which meets all of the following requirements:
  a. The validity of extension of the temporary use period of mountainous districts shall be reviewed by the Central Mountainous District Management Committee or the Local Mountainous District Management Committee according to the following categories:
  1) In the case of mountainous districts in State forest under the jurisdiction of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service: Central Mountainous District Management Committee;
  2) In case of mountainous districts of State forest, public forest, or private forest not under the jurisdiction of the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service: Regional Mountainous District Management Committee;
  b. The total of the initial period for temporary use of mountainous districts, the extended period for temporary use of mountainous districts temporarily granted, and the period to be extended shall not exceed 30 years.
  (4) Notwithstanding the main clause of Article 19(2) which is applied mutatis mutandis under paragraph (2), where a person who intends to obtain permission for extension under paragraph (3) intends to extend the period for temporary use of a mountainous district for the purpose of installation of ancillary facilities under Article 15-2(4)3 of the Act until the date such period is extended under paragraph (3), he or she may file a notification of modifications beyond the initial temporary use period of the mountainous district. <Added on Dec. 31, 2014; Jun. 21, 2016; Jun. 2, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 7, 2010]

Article 19 (Permission for extension of period for conversion of mountainous districts) (1) A person who intends to obtain permission to extend a mountainous district conversion period or file a notification for the modification of a mountainous district conversion period under Article 17(2) of the Act shall submit an application form for permission to extend the mountainous district conversion period or a notification form for the modification of mountainous district conversion period, along with documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. no later than 10 days before the expiration of the mountainous district conversion period; provided, if the person fails to apply for the extension permit or fails to file the modification notification by 10 days before the expiration of the mountainous district conversion period, the person shall submit an application form for the permission of extension of the mountainous district conversion period or a notification form for the modification of mountainous district conversion, stating the grounds for the delay; yet, after the expiration of the mountainous district conversion period, the conversion of mountainous district shall not be allowed, until the permission is granted for the extension of a mountainous district conversion period, or the notification of modification thereof is accepted. <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. receives a written application for permission for the extension of period for conversion of mountainous districts or a written notification of modifications of conversion of mountainous districts under paragraph (1), and deems it appropriate to extend or modify the conversion, he or she shall grant permission for the extension or accept the notification of modifications, within the range that the total of the existing extended period and the newly requested extension period does not exceed the initial period for conversion of mountainous districts; provided, in any of the following cases, the period for conversion of mountainous districts may be extended beyond the initial conversion period: <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Jul. 27, 2007; Apr. 20, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Sep. 24, 2014>
  1. Deleted; <Dec. 7, 2010>
  2. Deleted; <Dec. 7, 2010>
  3. In cases of administrative dispositions under which permission or notification for conversion of mountainous districts are deemed granted or filed under other statutes, and the relevant statutes separately prescribe the extension of the period for such dispositions;
  4. If the project cannot be completed within the extension period due to any of the following reasons:
  a. Natural disaster;
  b. In cases where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. determines that there is temporary management deterioration, lack of funds, or other unavoidable reasons.
  (3) Where permission is granted for the extension of period for conversion of mountainous districts under paragraph (2), the permit certificate of conversion of mountainous districts in the form prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, shall be issued to the applicant; provided, where the applicant or reporter is required to make a restoration deposit in advance under Article 38 of the Act, the extension shall be permitted or the notification of modifications shall be accepted after confirming such deposit. <Added on Aug. 5, 2005; Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 20 (Standards for permission for conversion of mountainous district) (1) "Ratio prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18(2)1 of the Act means 10/100. <Added on Dec. 7, 2010>
  (2) In Article 18(2)2 of the Act, "collective mountainous districts for forestry use prescribed by Presidential Decree" means mountainous districts for forestry use with an area of 30,000 square meters or more in 1 parcel or 2 or more adjacent parcels. <Added on Dec. 7, 2010>
  (3) In granting permission for mountainous district conversion, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. may attach the following conditions in accordance with Article 18(3) of the Act: <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Oct. 30, 2018; Mar. 11, 2025>
  1. Where the area of the mountainous districts to be converted is 100,000 square meters or more, the alteration of land characteristics shall be in phases, or interim restoration shall be conducted for the parts where the alteration has been completed;
  2. A shielding forest shall be created to maintain the landscape of mountainous districts;
  3. Soil and sand generated during project implementation shall be removed outside the project implementation area;
  4. Projects shall be implemented for the development of forest resources, such as afforestation and forest tending, in the areas that are conserved as forests;
  5. Disaster prevention facilities, such as soil runoff prevention facilities, rockfall prevention facilities, retaining walls, dams, sedimentation basins, and drainage facilities (hereinafter referred to as "disaster prevention facilities"), shall be installed;
  6. Other conditions determined and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. for the purpose of maintaining forest functions, conservation of mountain landscapes, etc.
  (4) In Article 18(4) of the Act, "mountainous districts exceeding the area prescribed by Presidential Decree" means a mountainous district exceeding 500,000 square meters. <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010>
  (5) In Article 18(4) of the Act, "where conservation mountainous districts are included in excess of an area prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the cases where conservation mountainous districts of 500,000 square meters or more are included. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012>
  (6) The scope of application of the permission criteria for conversion of mountainous districts under the main clause of Article 18(5) of the Act, and the detailed criteria by project type and scale shall be as specified in Appendix 4, and the permission criteria regarding the area of mountainous districts shall be as specified in Appendix 4-2. <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010>
  (7) Where it is deemed necessary for the use and conservation of mountainous districts due to regional conditions, pursuant to the proviso of Article 18(5) of the Act, the following permission criteria may be strengthened by Municipal Ordinance of the relevant local government, or relaxed within a range not exceeding 10 percent (20 percent in the case of a depopulation region as defined in subparagraph 12 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Local Autonomy and Decentralization, and Balanced Regional Development): <Added on Dec. 7, 2010; Sep. 24, 2014; Jun. 2, 2020; Jan. 7, 2025; Jul. 8, 2025>
  1. Standards for permission under subparagraph 1e6) of Appendix 4, subparagraph 2a and c1) and 3) of that Appendix;
  2. Standards for permission regarding the area of a mountainous district specified in Appendix 4-2.

Article 20-2 (Targets of feasibility study on conversion of mountainous district) (1) In the main clause of Article 18-2(1) of the Act, the "scale prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the scale according to the following categories. In such cases, in cases of requesting a consultation on modifications or seeking permission for modifications, only the area of mountainous districts subject to modifications shall be used as the standards: <Amended on Oct. 30, 2018>
  1. In cases of consultations or consultations on modifications under Article 8(1) of the Act: 300,000 square meters;
  2. In cases of permission for conversion of a mountainous district or permission for modifications thereof, or permission for temporary use of mountainous districts or permission for modifications under Article 14(1) or Article 15-2(1) of the Act (including cases where permission for conversion of mountainous districts or permission for modifications, or permission for temporary use of mountainous districts or permission for modifications are deemed granted under other statutes): Scale according to the following categories:
  a. In cases of installing wind power generation facilities or tramway facilities: 660 square meters;
  b. In other cases: 300,000 square meters.
  (2) In the main clause of Article 18-2(1) of the Act, "a specialized mountainous district institution prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the KFCA and the Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation. <Amended on Aug. 8 22, 2012; Dec. 30, 2016; Dec. 16, 2021; Jan. 30, 2026>
  (3) In the proviso of Article 18-2(1) of the Act, "cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the following cases: <Amended on Dec. 31, 2014; Jun. 2, 2017>
  1. In cases of installing agricultural, forestry, and fishery facilities and disaster prevention and recovery facilities; provided, reclamation under the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act and grassland development under the Grassland Act shall be excluded;
  2. In cases of requesting a consultation or consultation on modifications under Article 8(1) of the Act while the area of mountainous districts for conversion or temporary use has not been determined;
  3. Where a person who requests a consultation or consultation on modifications under Article 8(1) of the Act has undergone a feasibility study on the conversion of mountainous districts, and seeks to obtain permission for conversion of mountainous districts, permission for modifications thereof, or permission for temporary use of mountainous districts, or permission for modifications thereof, under Article 14 or 15-2 of the Act (including administrative dispositions by which permission for conversion of mountainous districts or permission for modifications thereof, or permission for temporary use of mountainous districts or permission for modifications thereof is deemed granted under other statues).
  [This Article Added on Dec. 7, 2010]
  [Previous Article 20-2 moved to Article 20-5 <Dec. 7, 2010>]

Article 20-3 (Procedures and standards for feasibility study on conversion of mountainous districts) (1) A person who intends to undergo a feasibility study on the conversion of mountainous districts under Article 18-2(1) of the Act shall submit an application form for a feasibility study on the conversion of mountainous districts to the KFCA or the Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation by attaching documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 30, 2016; Dec. 16, 2021; Jan. 30, 2026>
  (2) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (1), the KFCA or Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation shall conduct a feasibility study on the conversion of mountainous districts in accordance with the standards set forth in Appendix 4-3, after confirming that the fees notified under paragraph (5) has been paid. In such case, the survey and analysis of mountainous district and elevation, etc. shall be conducted in accordance with the following standards: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 30, 2016; Jun. 2, 2017; Oct. 30, 2018; Nov. 27, 2018; Jul. 2, 2019; Dec. 16, 2021; Jan. 30, 2026>
  1. Forestry survey and analysis of factors such as standing timber volume and the age of forest shall be conducted by a forest management technician of senior or higher level as prescribed in Appendix 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Promotion and Management of Forestry Technology;
  2. Investigation and analysis of elevation and average slope, etc.: To be conducted by a forest engineering technician according to Appendix 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Forestry Technology Promotion and Management Act or a person holding national technical qualifications in the fields of forestry, civil engineering, and surveying as prescribed by the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs;
  3. Other investigations and analysis of mountain landscape maintenance and disaster prevention: To be conducted by experts in the fields of forestry, environment, and mountain areas as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
  (3) In cases where it is necessary for the investigation and analysis under paragraph (2)1, KFCA or Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation may request data on forest fire occurrence, thinning, or logging history from the head of the relevant administrative agency. In such cases, the head of the relevant administrative agency who received the request shall provide the relevant data to the KFCA or the Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation within 30 days from the date of the request, unless there is a special reason not to do so. <Added on Dec. 30, 2016; Dec. 16, 2021; Jan. 30, 2026>
  (4) When the KFCA or the Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation completes the feasibility study on the conversion of mountainous districts under paragraph (2), they may hold an explanation session on the study results to collect opinions from local residents and other stakeholders. <Added on Dec. 17, 2013; Dec. 30, 2016; Dec. 16, 2021; Jan. 30, 2026>
  (5) Upon receipt of an application for the feasibility survey on the conversion of mountainous district under paragraph (1), the KFCA or Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation shall calculate the service fees based on the standards determined and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, which reflect the actual cost-plus method prescribed in Article 31(2) of the Engineering Industry Promotion Act, the wage rates surveyed by relevant organizations, and others, and shall notify the applicant thereof. <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; Nov. 11, 2015; Dec. 30, 2016; Jan. 5, 2021; Dec. 16, 2021; Jan. 30, 2026>
  (6) The Administrator of Korea Forest Service shall determine and publicly notify matters necessary for the notification, payment, and refund of fees under paragraph (5). <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; Dec. 30, 2016>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 7, 2010]

Article 20-4 (Disclosure of results of feasibility study on conversion of mountainous districts) Where the KFCA or the Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation has notified the results of feasibility study of the conversion of mountainous districts under Article 18-2(3) of the Act, it shall publish the results of the study on the website of the KFCA or Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation within 10 days from the date of notification, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 30, 2016; Dec. 16, 2021; Jan. 30, 2026>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 7, 2010]

Article 20-5 (Preservation of documents of feasibility study on conversion of mountainous districts) (1) In Article 18-2(4) of the Act, "documents and other materials prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the following documents and materials: <Amended on Jul. 2, 2019>
  1. Documents on the results of feasibility study on the conversion of mountainous districts and their opinions;
  2. Forest survey and analysis documents on the stocking of trees, age of forest, etc. and supporting data therefor;
  3. Documents, drawings, and supporting data in the form of electronic media such as CD on survey and analysis data related to elevation, average slope, and other relevant factors;
  4. Documents for investigation and analysis of disaster prevention, etc. and their supporting data.
  (2) "Period prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18-2(4) of the Act means 3 years from the date a feasibility survey on the conversion of a mountainous district is completed.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 2, 2017]
  [Previous Article 20-5 moved to Article 20-6 <Jun. 2, 2017>]

Article 20-6 (Confirmation of conformity with standards for permission for conversion of mountainous districts) In the main clause, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 18-4(1) of the Act, "mountainous districts exceeding the area prescribed by Presidential Decree" means a mountainous district with an area of 30,000 square meters or more that is subject to consultation on designation of zones, etc. within mountainous districts under Article 8 of the Act, or permission for the conversion of mountainous districts or consultations thereon, under Article 14 of the Act. <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010>
  [This Article Added on Nov. 26, 2009]
  [This Article Moved from Article 20-5 <Jun. 2, 2017>]

Article 21 (Substitute forest resource development charges) (1) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. may, pursuant to Article 19 (2) 1 of the Act, grant permission for the conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district on the condition that substitute forest resource development charges are paid by the deadline specified in each of the following subparagraphs, starting from the date on which permission for such conversion or temporary use is granted (in cases where permission for such conversion or temporary use is deemed to have been granted or excluded pursuant to other Acts, the date on which the relevant administrative disposition is made): <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 11, 2025>
  1. When the amount to be paid is less than 10 million won: 20 to 30 days;
  2. When the amount to be paid is 10 million won or more but less than 50 million won: 30 days or more but less than 60 days;
  3. When the amount to be paid is 50 million won or more: 60 days or more but less than 90 days.
  (2) In cases where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. recognize that it is difficult to pay the substitute forest resource development charges in a lump sum in any of the following cases set forth in Article 19(2)2 of the Act, they may require the deposit of a performance guarantee and allow payment in installments over a period not exceeding 4 years, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs: <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Feb. 29, 2008; Jul. 24, 2008; Apr. 20, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; Nov. 16, 2011; Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 24, 2014; Oct. 30, 2018; Jun. 7, 2023>
  1. Where the State, a local government, a public corporation, a quasi-governmental institution, a local government-invested public corporation, or a local public agency intends to obtain permission for the conversion of mountainous districts under Article 14 of the Act, permission for temporary use of mountainous districts under Article 15-2 of the Act, or administrative dispositions under Article 19(1)3 of the Act (hereafter referred to as "permission, etc. for conversion of a mountainous district" in this paragraph) as a site for facilities of an industrial complex under subparagraph 8 of Article 2 of the Industrial Sites and Development Act;
  2. In cases where the executor under Article 11(1) of the Urban Development Act seeks to obtain permission for the conversion of mountainous districts, etc. as a site for an urban development project under Article 2(1)2 of that Act;
  3. In cases where a business entity under Article 55 of the Tourism Promotion Act seeks to obtain permission for conversion of a mountainous district for use as a tourist site under subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of that Act or as a facility site for a tourist complex under subparagraph 7 of that Article;
  4. In cases where an executor under Article 7 of the Housing Site Development Promotion Act seeks to obtain permission for the conversion of a mountainous district into a residential site under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of that Act;
  5. In cases where a person who intends to operate a small and medium-sized enterprise under Article 2(2) of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises wishes to obtain permission for the conversion of mountainous districts as a factory site for a small and medium-sized enterprise;
  6. In cases where the total payment amount of substitute forest resource development charges calculated in accordance with Article 19(8) of the Act is KRW 100 million or more.

Article 22 Deleted. <Jul. 27, 2007>

Article 23 (Reduction or exemption of substitute forest resource development charges) (1) The target and ratio of reduction or exemption of substitute forest resource development charges according to Article 19(5) of the Act are as per Appendix 5. <Amended on Oct. 30, 2018>
  (2) In the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 19(5) of the Act, "a period for reduction or exemption" means the period for review of the appropriateness of the target and rate of reduction or exemption of substitute forest resource development charges, and the period shall be 5 years. <Added on Oct. 30, 2018>
  (3) In order to review the appropriateness of the target and rate of reduction and exemption of substitute forest resource development charges every 5 years in accordance with paragraph (2), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall formulate a plan to adjust the target and rate of reduction or exemption of substitute forest resource development charges (hereinafter referred to as the "reduction or exemption plan") after deliberation by the Central Mountainous District Management Committee in accordance with Article 19(6) of the Act. <Added on Oct. 30, 2018>
  (4) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall formulate a reduction or exemption plan and notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies thereof no later than 1 year prior to the fifth anniversary from the date of formulation of the previous reduction or exemption plan. In such cases, the head of the relevant central administrative agency notified of the reduction or exemption plan shall submit his or her opinion to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service regarding the effects of the reduction or exemption of substitute forest resource development charges, necessity of maintaining the reduction or exemption target, etc. <Added on Oct. 30, 2018>
  (5) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall adjust the target and rate of reduction or exemption of substitute forest resource development charges in Appendix 5 according to the reduction or exemption plan formulated under paragraph (3). <Added on Oct. 30, 2018>
  (6) In Article 19(5)2 of the Act, "important industrial facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the facilities specified in subparagraph 2 of Appendix 5. <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010; Oct. 30, 2018>
  (7) In Article 19(5)3 of the Act, "installation of facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree or use for purposes prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the installation of facilities or use for purposes set forth in subparagraph 3 of Appendix 5 (excluding item i). <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010; Oct. 30, 2018>

Article 24 (Payment deadline and calculation standards for substitute forest resource development charges) (1) In cases where the amount of substitute forest resource development charges is determined under Article 19(8) and (10) of the Act, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. shall, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, issue a payment notice to the person liable to pay the substitute forest resource development charges, specifying the amount, due date, place of payment, and other relevant details. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Oct. 30, 2018; Mar. 11, 2025>
  (2) In cases where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. issues a payment notice in accordance with paragraph (1), he or she shall set a payment period of 20 to 90 days from the date of issuance of the payment notice and notify the person; provided, in cases where a person who is required to pay the substitute forest resource development charges applies for an extension of the period due to unavoidable reasons, the period may be extended only once within the scope of the initially notified payment period, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Substitute forest resource development charges shall be levied based on the following areas according to the type of permission for conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts, based on the area specified in the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010>
  1. In cases where a person intends to obtain permission for conversion of mountainous districts under Article 14 of the Act or permission for temporary use of mountainous districts under Article 15-2(1) of the Act: The area of the mountainous districts subject to permission for the conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts;
  2. In cases where a person intends to obtain administrative dispositions by which permission for conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts is deemed granted or excluded under other statutes: The area of mountainous districts by which permission for conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts is deemed granted or excluded according to the relevant administrative dispositions.
  (4) The amount of substitute forest resource development charges per unit area under Article 19 (8) of the Act shall be determined and publicly notified each year by Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, after deliberation by the Central Mountainous Districts Management Committee, taking into account the sum of the annual cost of planting Korean pine trees and forest tending costs for 10 years after planting, the assessed public values of forests, such as water source conservation, air purification, soil runoff prevention, and greenhouse gas absorption, and the individual public notice price of the relevant mountainous district, under the Act on the Public Announcement of Values and Appraisal of Real Estate. In such cases, under the latter part of Article 19 (8) of the Act, the amount may vary by mountainous district type or region, in accordance with the latter part of Article 19 (8) of the Act: <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Aug. 31, 2016; Oct. 30, 2018; Mar. 11, 2025>
  1. For a restricted area for conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts, an amount obtained by adding 100% to the amount per unit area;
  2. For a conservation mountainous district excluding the restricted areas for conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts, the amount shall be the amount per unit area plus 30/100;
  3. For a semi-conservation area, an amount per unit area for the semi-conservation area.
  (5) "payment service provider prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 19(11) of the Act means the following agencies: <Added on Oct. 17, 2017; Oct. 30, 2018>
  1. The Korea Financial Telecommunications and Clearings Institute established with permission from the Financial Services Commission under Article 32 of the Civil Act;
  2. An institution that performs payments using credit cards, debit cards, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "credit cards, etc.") through the information and communications networks, and that the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service designates and publicly notifies as a payment service provider, taking into consideration the facilities, business performance capabilities, capital size, etc.
  (6) The payment service provider may collect fees for vicarious payment from persons liable for payment within the extent not exceeding 10/1000 of the amount of payment in consideration for services for vicarious payment by credit card, etc. In such cases, the payment service provider shall obtain approval from the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service for the payment agency fees. <Added on Oct. 17, 2017>
  (7) In case of approval under the latter part of paragraph (6), the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service shall comprehensively consider the operating expenses and others of the payment service provider agency. <Added on Oct. 17, 2017>
  [Title amended on Oct. 17, 2017]

Article 25 Deleted. <Aug. 22, 2012>

Article 25-2 (Refund of substitute forest resource development charges) (1) In cases where there is an amount to be refunded in accordance with Article 19-2 of the Act among the amounts paid as substitute forest resource development charges, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. shall, without delay, determine the amount as a refund of substitute forest resource development charges, and notify a person who paid the substitute forest resource development charges, etc. thereof; provided, in cases where they issue an order to restore a mountainous district under Article 44(1)3 or 5 of the Act, notification shall be made after confirming whether the mountainous district has been restored. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Nov. 24, 2020>
  (2) Articles 17 and 18 of the Enforcement Decree of the Management of the National Funds Act shall apply to the refund procedures and notification of substitute forest resource development charges. <Amended on Oct. 30, 2018>
  (3) Where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. gives notice of a substitute forest resource development charges in creating forest replacement resources pursuant to paragraph (1), they shall determine and notify the additional amount of refund interest, which shall be calculated by multiplying the amount of the refund by the interest rate for national tax refunds as provided in Article 43-3(2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on National Taxes, for the period from the day following the date specified in any of the following subparagraphs to the date on which the refund is determined, together with the refund amount:
  1. In the case of subparagraphs 1 and 2 of Article 19-2 of the Act, the date on which the substitute forest resource development charges are paid;
  2. In the case specified in Article 19-2(1)3 of the Act, any of the following dates:
  a. In cases where a final disposition has been made to deny an administrative disposition necessary for the implementation of the intended project to use the mountainous district for any other purposes, the date on which the substitute forest resource development charges are paid;
  b. In cases where a final disposition has been made to revoke the administrative disposition necessary for the implementation of the intended project to use the mountainous district for any other purposes, the date on which the permission for the conversion of the mountainous district or the permission for temporary use of the mountainous district is deemed to have been revoked under Article 16(2) of the Act;
  3. In the case of Article 19-2(1)4 of the Act, the date on which the period for the conversion of a mountainous district expires;
  4. In the case of Article 19-2(1)5 of the Act, the date on which the permission for the conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district is revoked;
  5. In the case of Article 19-2(1)6 of the Act, any of the following dates:
  a. In cases where an administrative disposition is not granted under which permission for conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district is deemed granted or excluded, the date of payment of substitute forest resource development charges;
  b. In cases where an administrative disposition by which permission for conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district is deemed granted or excluded is revoked, the date the relevant administrative disposition is revoked;
  6. In the case of Article 19-2(1)7 of the Act, the date of receipt of permission for modifications or the date of receipt of notification of modifications;
  7. In the case of Article 19-2(1)8 of the Act, the date of confirmation of the use of a mountainous district as a purpose eligible for a reduction or exemption of the substitute forest resource development charges;
  8. In the case specified in each subparagraph of Article 19-2(2) of the Act, the date of payment of the substitute forest resource development charges.
  (4) In determining the refund of substitute forest resource development charges and the additional refund interest under paragraphs (1) through (3), if the substitute forest resource development charges are paid in two or more installments, the refund shall be made from the substitute forest resource development charges that are paid most recently. <Amended on Nov. 24, 2020>
  (5) In Article 19-2(1)7 of the Act, "reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree" means where the area of a mountainous district subject to the substitute reforestation charges has decreased due to a measurement error. <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010; Oct. 30, 2018>
  (6) In Article 19-2(1)8 of the Act, "reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the cases where the use for purposes eligible for reduction or exemption of substitute forest resource development charges under this Act or other statutes has been confirmed prior to conducting a restoration completion inspection under Article 42 of the Act (limited to cases where the use for relevant purpose has been confirmed during the period of permission for conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts under Article 14 or 15-2(1) of the Act). <Amended on Oct. 30, 2018>
  (7) Where the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc., offset in advance the costs necessary for the restoration of mountainous districts from the refund of substitute forest resource development charges and additional refund interests under paragraphs (1) and (3) (excluding subparagraph 8), in accordance with the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 19-2(1), the offset amount shall be made as restoration deposit under Article 38(1) of the Act. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Oct. 30, 2018>
  (8) If a person who has received a refund after deducting the substitute forest resource development charges according to the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 19-2(1) of the Act intends to convert the mountainous district including the same area again within 10 years, he or she shall pay the amount excluding the deducted substitute forest resource development charges as the substitute forest resource development charges. <Amended on Oct. 30, 2018; Nov. 24, 2020>
  (9) Deleted. <Oct. 30, 2018>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 25-3 Deleted. <Dec. 7, 2010>

Article 26 (Approval for change of use) (1) In Article 21(1)1 of the Act, "Where he or she intends to use the land ... for another purpose, within a period prescribed by Presidential Decree" means any of the following cases: <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Jul. 27, 2007; Oct. 29, 2008; Dec. 7, 2010; Nov. 11, 2015; Jun. 2, 2017>
  1. In cases where a person who has obtained permission for the conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district, or who has filed a notification thereof, for the purpose of installing a facility, changes the purpose of use of the facility within 5 years from any of the following dates:
  a. The date of obtaining approval for use under Article 22 of the Building Act;
  b. Where approval, notification, or inspection for use of the relevant facility is required under applicable statutes and regulations, other than the case referred to in item a, the date on which such approval, notification, or inspection for use is obtained;
  c. In cases where other applicable statutes or regulations do not stipulate administrative procedures necessary for using the relevant facilities, the date on which the person who has performed the installation works completes the installation of relevant facilities;
  2. Where a person who has obtained permission for the conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district, or who has filed a notification thereof, for a purpose other than the installation of a facility, intends to change the purpose of use of the relevant mountainous district within 5 years from any of the following dates:
  a. In cases where restoration is required in accordance with Article 39(1) and (2) of the Act, the date of inspection of completion of the restoration works in accordance with Article 42(1) of the Act;
  b. In cases where the obligation to perform restoration is exempted under Article 39(3)2 of the Act, the date on which the obligation is exempted.
  (2) In Article 21(1)2 of the Act, "within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree" means within 5 years from the date of receiving approval for use in accordance with Article 22 of the Building Act. <Added on Jul. 27, 2007; Apr. 20, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010>
  (3) The amount of substitute forest resource development charges payable under Article 21(2) of the Act shall be calculated in accordance with the following formula: <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007>
  ┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ (Area of conversion of mountainous district × amount per unit area at the time of imposition × applicable reduction or exemption rate at the time of approval of modification) │
  │ - Substitute forest resource development charges already paid │
  └───────────────────────────────────────┘
  (4) Articles 21, 23, and 24 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the decision to impose substitute forest resource development charges under paragraph (3), the payment notice, and the payment procedures, etc. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Dec. 7, 2010; Oct. 30, 2018>
  (5) In accordance with Article 21(3) of the Act, the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, etc. shall approve a change of use only when it meets the following criteria: <Added on Jul. 27, 2007; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Oct. 30, 2018; Jun. 2, 2020>
  1. In cases of change in land use following a notification of the conversion of mountainous districts: The change shall conform to the scope of the facilities and activities subject to the notification of conversion of mountainous districts, as well as to the location and installation conditions specified in Article 15(2) of the Act;
  2. In cases of change of land use following permission for the conversion of mountainous districts: It shall conform to the criteria for permission for conversion of mountainous districts under Article 18 of the Act;
  3. In cases of change of land use following permission for and notification of temporary use of mountainous districts: The change shall conform to the scope of facilities and activities subject to permission for and notification of temporary use of mountainous districts, the location and installation conditions under Article 15-2(5) of the Act.
  [Title Amended on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 26-2 (Special cases on the National Land Planning and Utilization Act) (1) "Period prescribed by Presidential Decree" under Article 21-2 of the Act means the period from the date specified in subparagraph 1 to the date specified in subparagraph 2; provided, where, before 5 years have passed from the date specified in subparagraph 1, an urban or Gun management plan regarding a change in the zoning of the relevant land has been determined in line with a revision of the urban or Gun management plan in accordance with Article 34(1) of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act , the period shall be from the date specified in subparagraph 1 to the date on which the topographic map of the urban or Gun management plan is publicly notified under Article 32(4) of that Act:
  1. A day on which a building or other facilities on the land currently or formerly used in a project for the conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district falls under any of the items of Article 26(1)1;
  2. The date on which the topographic map of an urban or Gun management plan determined in accordance with the first revision of an urban or Gun management plan under Article 34(1) of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act is publicly notified under Article 32(4) of that Act after 5 years have elapsed from the date specified in subparagraph 1.
  (2) Where a preserved mountainous district under Article 76(5)3 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act is reclassified to a land category other than forest land in accordance with permission for conversion of a mountainous district under Article 14 of the Act, or notification of conversion of a mountainous district under Article 15 (including administrative dispositions deemed to constitute permission for conversion of a mountainous district or notification of conversion of a mountainous district under other statutes), the restrictions on the use, type, and scale of buildings or other facilities on the land that is or has been used for the intended project for such conversion of a mountainous district during the period under paragraph (1), notwithstanding Article 76 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act, shall be governed by the standards under Article 26(5)1 and 2; provided, the same shall not apply where the restrictions under Article 76 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act are determined as more relaxed than the standards under Article 26(5)1 and 2.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 2, 2017]
  [Previous Article 26-2 moved to Article 26-3 <Jun. 2, 2017>]

Article 26-3 (Restrictions on changes in land category of mountainous districts) In subparagraph 2 of Article 21-3 of the Act, "cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as a project operator's application for merger of land, so as to promote an urban development project prescribed in Article 86 of the Act on the Establishment and Management of Spatial Data" means cases where a project implementer applies for a land merger in order to smoothly promote an urban development project, etc. under Article 86 of the Act on the Establishment and Management of Spatial Data. <Amended on Jun. 2, 2017>
  [This Article Added on Sep. 25, 2015]
  [Moved from Article 26-2 <Jun. 2, 2017>]

SECTION 4 Mountainous District Management Committee

Article 27 (Matters for deliberation by the Central Mountainous District Management Committee) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 22(1)3 of the Act means any of the following matters: <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Feb. 1, 2007; Jul. 27, 2007; Dec. 7, 2010; Sep. 24, 2014; Oct. 30, 2018; Oct. 4, 2022; Mar. 28, 2023; Mar. 11, 2025>
  1. Matters for which consultation has been requested to the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service in accordance with Article 8 of the Act and which have been submitted to the Central Mountainous District Management Committee in accordance with Article 7(5);
  2. Deleted; <Sep. 24, 2014>
  3. Matters related to permission for temporary use of a mountainous district under Article 15-2(1) of the Act that includes a conservation mountainous district with an area of 500,000 square meters or more;
  4. Matters related to the validity of the target and rate of reduction or exemption of substitute forest resource development charges under Article 19(6) of the Act;
  4-2. Matters related to the determination and public notification of the amount per unit area of substitute forest resource development charges under Article 19(8) of the Act;
  5. Matters related to the designation of quarry complexes under Article 29 of the Act (applies only to cases designated by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service);
  5-2. Matters related to the standards for calculating restoration costs pursuant to Article 38(5) of the Act;
  6. Matters related to the relaxation of the standards for approval of a design plan for restoration under Article 40(4) of the Act;
  7. Matters related to the relaxation of restrictions on the area of sites in mountainous districts for forestry use under Article 12(14);
  7-2. Matters related to the validity of the extension of the temporary use period of mountainous districts under Article 18-4(3)2a;
  8. Matters related to relaxation of criteria for permission specified in Note 4 of Appendix 3-2;
  9. Matters related to relaxation of criteria for permission specified in Note 5 of Appendix 4;
  10. Matters related to relaxation of criteria for permission specified in Note 1 of Appendix 4-2;
  10-2. Matters that require deliberation by the Central Mountainous District Management Committee as prescribed in other statutes and regulations;
  11. In cases where it is intended to designate the areas, etc. for the purpose of developing a mountainous district of public interest or nearby mountainous district, matters deemed necessary by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service for the conservation of the forest ecosystem and mountain landscape, as well as other matters the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service deems necessary.

Article 28 (Composition of Central Mountainous District Management Committee) (1) The Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall be composed of no more than 70 members, including 1 chairperson and 2 vice-chairpersons. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Jul. 24, 2008; Dec. 7, 2010; Oct. 4, 2022>
  (2) The chairperson of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall be the Deputy Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, the vice chairperson shall be one person elected from among the committee members and a Grade 3 public official or a member of the Senior Executive Service of the Korea Forest Service in charge of mountainous district management affairs. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007>
  (3) The chairperson of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall represent the Committee and have general supervision and control of its affairs. <Amended on Jul. 2, 2019>
  (4) Where the chairperson of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee is unable to perform his or her duties due to any unavoidable cause, such duties shall be performed, in the following order of priority, by: the vice-chairperson elected from among the committee members; a Grade III public official of the Korea Forest Service in charge of the management of mountainous districts, or a public official belonging to the Senior Executive Service; and a Committee member designated in advance by the chairperson. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007>
  (5) The members of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall be appointed or commissioned by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service from among those specified in any of the following subparagraphs: In such cases, at least 1 member recommended by a civic group (referring to a non-profit private organization as defined in Article 2 of the Assistance for Non-Profit, Non-Governmental Organizations Act) and at least 1 female member shall be included: <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; Jul. 24, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Oct. 4, 2022; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Not more than 7 persons designated by the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, from among public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service of the relevant central administrative agencies, such as the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Ministry of Climate, Energy, and Environment, and the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  2. Up to 60 people from among those who have abundant knowledge and experience in conservation and use of mountainous districts, environment, national land and urban planning, stone industry, etc., and who fall under any of the following categories and have gone through the recommendation or public offering process of relevant organizations such as related academic societies and associations or relevant central administrative agencies:
  a. A person who holds or has held a position higher than assistant professor at a university as defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
  b. A person who has received a doctorate and has at least 3 years of research or practical experience;
  c. A person who has obtained a master's degree and has more than 9 years of research or practical experience;
  d. A person who has acquired a technical engineer qualification under the National Technical Qualifications Act and has at least 3 years of practical experience;
  e. Other persons recognized by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service as having knowledge and experience equivalent to those specified in any of the items a through d.
  (6) The term of office of a member who is not a public official shall be 2 years; provided, in the case of a substitute member, the term of office shall be the remaining term of office of the predecessor. <Amended on Apr. 6, 2011>

Article 29 (Operation of Central Mountainous District Management Committee) (1) The chairperson of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall convene and preside over meetings of the Committee.
  (2) The chairperson of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall convene a meeting without delay when requested by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or at least one-third of the committee members.
  (3) A majority of the 15 members designated by the chairperson of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall constitute a quorum, and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present. <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008>
  (4) If the Central Mountainous District Management Committee deems it necessary, it may request the heads of relevant administrative agencies to submit necessary data, and may hear explanations from experts with abundant knowledge in the conservation and use of mountainous districts. <Added on Jul. 27, 2007>
  (5) The heads of relevant administrative agencies may attend the Central Mountainous District Management Committee and speak on matters related to the conservation and use of mountainous district. <Added on Jul. 27, 2007>
  (6) Except as otherwise provided in this Decree, matters necessary for the operation of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution by the Committee. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007>

Article 29-2 (Establishment and operation of sectoral committees of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee) (1) In order to ensure efficient deliberation by the Central Mountainous District Management Committee, the following sectoral committees shall be established within the Central Mountainous District Management Committee, and the matters for deliberation shall be as follows: <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012; Dec. 17, 2013; Sep. 24, 2014; Oct. 30, 2018; Oct. 4, 2022; Mar. 28, 2023; Mar. 11, 2025>
  1. Sectoral committee I:
  a. Formulation and amendment of the master plan;
  b. Designation, modification, and revocation of designation of conservation mountainous districts under Articles 5 and 6 of the Act;
  c. Designation and revocation of restricted areas for conversion or temporary use of mountainous districts under Articles 9 and 11 of the Act;
  d. Matters related to the eligibility for reduction and exemption of the substitute forest resource development charges and the appropriateness of the reduction and exemption rate thereof under Article 19(6) of the Act;
  e. Matters concerning the determination and public notification of the amount per unit area of substitute forest resource development charges under Article 19(8) of the Act;
  f. Other matters delegated by the Central Mountain Management Committee;
  2. Sectoral committee II:
  a. Deleted; <Dec. 17, 2013>
  b. Consultation on designation of zones, etc. in mountainous districts in accordance with Article 8 of the Act;
  c. Deleted; <Sep. 24, 2014>
  d. Matters related to permission for temporary use of a mountainous district under Article 15-2(1) of the Act that includes a conservation mountainous district with an area of 500,000 square meters or more;
  e. Matters related to the validity of conversion of a mountainous district under Article 18(4) of the Act;
  f. Matters related to the designation of quarry complexes under Article 29 of the Act (only applicable to cases designated by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service);
  g. Matters concerning the standards for calculating restoration costs pursuant to Article 38(5) of the Act;
  h. Matters related to the relaxation of the standards for approval of design plans for restoration under Article 40(4) of the Act;
  i. Matters related to the relaxation of restrictions on the site area in mountainous districts for forestry use under Article 12(14);
  j. Matters related to the validity of the extension of the period for temporary use of a mountainous district under Article 18-4(3)2a;
  k. Matters related to relaxation of permission criteria under Note 4 of Appendix 3-2;
  l. Matters related to relaxation of criteria for permission specified in Note 5 of Appendix 4;
  m. Matters related to relaxation of permission criteria under Note 1 of Appendix 4-2;
  n. Matters concerning the standards for calculating costs for disaster prevention, etc. pursuant to Article 23(1) of the Special Act on Management of Mountainous Districts North of the Civilian Control Line;
  o. Other matters delegated by the Central Mountain Management Committee;
  3. Sectoral committee III:
  a. Matters that require deliberation by the Central Mountainous District Management Committee as prescribed in Enforcement Decree of the Stone Industry Promotion Act;
  b. Other matters delegated by the Central Mountain Management Committee;
  (2) Each sectoral committee shall be comprised of not more than 25 members, including one chairperson. <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008>
  (3) The chairperson of each sectoral committee shall be the Deputy Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
  (4) The members of each sectoral committee shall be elected by the Central Mountainous District Management Committee from among its members, and any civilian member of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall not serve on 2 or more sectoral committees.
  (5) A majority of the 15 members designated by the chairperson of a sectoral committee shall constitute a quorum, and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present. <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008>
  (6) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the chairperson of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee may coordinate some of the duties allocated to each sectoral committee, if necessary for efficient examination. <Added on Jul. 24, 2008>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 29-3 (Exclusion and recusal of member of Central Mountainous District Management Committee) (1) If a member of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee falls under any of the following subparagraphs, he or she shall be excluded from deliberation and resolution on the agenda item subject to relevant deliberation:
  1. Where a committee member is directly involved in the relevant agenda item under deliberation through services or other means;
  2. Where a committee member is a stakeholder in the relevant agenda item under deliberation.
  (2) When a member of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee falls under any of the grounds listed in each subparagraph of paragraph (1), they may voluntarily recuse themselves from deliberation and resolution on the relevant agenda item, and shall notify the secretary of the recusal at least 1 day prior to the meeting date.
  (3) Deleted. <Nov. 11, 2015>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 30 (Expert committee members and secretaries) (1) In order to investigate and research important matters related to management of mountainous district and others, expert members may be assigned to the Central Mountainous District Management Committee.
  (2) The expert committee members may attend a meeting to state their opinions, upon the request by the chairperson or the Central Mountainous District Management Committee.
  (3) The expert committee members shall be appointed or commissioned by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service from among those with extensive knowledge and experience in the conservation and use of mountainous district, environment, national land and urban planning, and the stone industry. <Amended on Oct. 4, 2022>
  (4) The Central Mountainous District Management Committee shall have 1 secretary, who shall be appointed by the chairperson.

Article 30-2 (Matters for deliberation by the Regional Mountainous District Management Committee) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 22(2)2 of the Act means any of the following matters: <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Dec. 17, 2013; Sep. 24, 2014; Oct. 30, 2018; Mar. 28, 2023>
  1. Formulation and revision of regional plans;
  2. Matters for which consultation has been requested to the Mayor/Do Governor in accordance with Article 8 of the Act and which have been submitted to the local mountainous district management committee in accordance with Article 7(5);
  3. Permission for extracting earth and stones under Article 25(1) of the Act;
  4. Matters related to the designation of quarry complexes under Article 29 of the Act (excluding cases designated by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service);
  4-2. Matters related to the relaxation of the criteria for approval of design plans for restoration under Article 40(4) of the Act;
  4-3. Matters related to the validity of extension of the period for temporary use of a mountainous district under Article 18-4(3)2a;
  4-4. Matters related to the relaxation of criteria for permission under Note 4 of Appendix 3-2;
  5. Matters related to the relaxation of criteria for permission under Note 4 of Appendix 4;
  6. Matters related to relaxation of criteria for permission under Note 1 of Appendix 4-2.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 7, 2010]

Article 31 (Establishment and operation of regional mountainous district management committee) (1) The regional mountainous district management committee shall be composed of no more than 50 members, including 1 chairperson and 2 vice-chairpersons. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Jul. 24, 2008; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012>
  (2) The chairperson of the regional mountainous district management committee shall be the Deputy Mayor or Vice Governor of the Special Metropolitan City, a Metropolitan City, a Special Self-Governing City, a Do, or a Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do") who is in charge of mountainous district management affairs, and the vice chairperson shall be a person elected from among and by the committee members. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012>
  (3) The chairperson of the regional mountainous district management committee shall represent the committee, and have general supervision and control of its affairs, and convene and preside over meetings of the committee. <Amended on Jul. 2, 2019>
  (4) Where the chairperson of a regional mountainous district management committee is unable to perform his or her duties due to any unavoidable cause, the vice chairperson and a committee member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on his or her behalf in the order named.
  (5) The chairperson of a regional mountainous district management committee shall convene meetings without delay, upon a request by the Mayor/Do Governor or by at least 1/3 of the members thereof.
  (6) The members of a regional mountainous district management committee shall be appointed or commissioned by the Mayor/Do Governor from among the following persons: In such cases, at least 1 member recommended by a civic group (referring to a non-profit, non-governmental organization as defined in Article 2 of the Assistance for Non-Profit, Non-Governmental Organizations Act) and at least 1 female member shall be included: <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Jul. 24, 2008>
  1. Not more than 7 public officials of Grade IV or higher in charge of affairs in the fields of agriculture, forestry, environment, construction, urban planning, and fire-fighting;
  2. Up to 40 people from among those who have abundant knowledge and experience in the conservation and use of mountainous districts, environment, national land and urban planning, etc., and who fall under any of the following categories and have gone through the recommendation or public offering process of the head of a related organization such as a related academic society or association or a related administrative agency:
  a. A person who holds or has held a position higher than assistant professor at a university as defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
  b. A person who has received a doctorate and has at least 3 years of research or practical experience;
  c. A person who has obtained a master's degree and has more than 9 years of research or practical experience;
  d. A person who has acquired a technical engineer qualification under the National Technical Qualifications Act and has at least 3 years of practical experience;
  e. Any other person deemed by the Mayor/Do Governor to have knowledge and experience equivalent to those specified in items a through d.
  (7) The term of office of a committee member who is not a public official shall be 2 years; provided, in the case of a substitute member, the term of office shall be the remaining term of office of the predecessor. <Amended on Apr. 6, 2011>
  (8) A majority of the 15 members designated by the chairperson of a regional mountainous district management committee shall constitute a quorum, and any decision thereof shall require the concurring vote of at least a majority of those present. <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008>
  (9) Expert members may be assigned to each regional mountainous district management committee, who shall be appointed or commissioned by the competent Mayor/Do Governor from among persons who have abundant knowledge and experience in the conservation and use of mountainous districts, the environment, national land and urban planning, etc.
  (10) A regional mountainous district management committee shall have 1 secretary, who shall be appointed by the chairperson.
  (11) Except as otherwise provided in this Decree, matters necessary for the operation of the regional mountainous district management committees shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution by the Committee.

Article 31-2 (Establishment and operation of sectoral committees of regional mountainous district management committee) (1) In order to ensure efficient deliberation by the regional mountainous district management committee, the following sectoral committees may be established within the regional mountainous district management committee, and the matters for deliberation shall be as follows: <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008; Dec. 7, 2010; Sep. 24, 2014; Oct. 30, 2018, Mar. 28, 2023>
  1. Sectoral committee I:
  a. Formulation and revision of regional plans;
  b. Deleted; <Dec. 7, 2010>
  c. Other matters delegated by the regional mountainous district management committee;
  2. Sectoral committee II:
  a. Deleted; <Dec. 7, 2010>
  b. Validity of permission for extraction of earth and stones under Article 25 of the Act;
  c. Matters related to the designation of quarry complexes under Article 29 of the Act (excluding cases designated by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service);
  d. Matters related to the relaxation of the standards for approval of design plans for restoration under Article 40(4) of the Act;
  e. Matters related to the validity of the extension of the period for temporary use of a mountainous district under Article 18-4(3)2a;
  f. Matters related to relaxation of permission criteria under Note 4 of Appendix 3-2;
  g. Matters related to relaxation of permission criteria under Note 4 of Appendix 4;
  h. Matters related to relaxation of permission criteria under Note 1 of Appendix 4-2;
  i. Other matters delegated by the regional mountainous district management committee.
  (2) In the case of establishing a sectoral committee under paragraph (1), each subcommittee shall be composed of no more than 25 members, including 1 chairperson. <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008>
  (3) The chairperson of each sectoral committee shall be the Deputy Mayor or Vice Governor of City/Do who is in charge of mountain management affairs.
  (4) The members of each sectoral committee shall be elected by the regional mountainous district management committee from among its members, and among the members of the regional mountainous district management committee, civilian members may not serve on 2 or more sectoral committees.
  (5) A majority of the 15 members designated by the chairperson of a sectoral committee shall constitute a quorum, and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present. <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008>
  (6) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the chairperson of a regional mountainous district management committee may, if necessary for efficient examination, adjust some of the work assigned to each sectoral committee. <Added on Jul. 24, 2008>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 31-3 (Exclusion and recusal of members of regional mountainous district management committee) (1) Where a member of a regional mountainous district management committee falls under any of the following, he or she shall be excluded from the deliberation and resolution on the relevant agenda item subject to deliberation:
  1. Where a committee member is directly involved in the relevant agenda item under deliberation through services or other means;
  2. Where a committee member is a stakeholder in the relevant agenda item under deliberation.
  (2) Where a member of a regional mountainous district management committee falls under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), he or she may voluntarily recuse himself or herself from deliberation and resolution on the relevant agenda item, and notify the secretary thereof by no later than 1 day before the date of the meeting.
  (3) Deleted. <Nov. 11, 2015>
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 31-4 (Grounds for disqualification) (1) A person who falls under any of the subparagraphs of Article 33 of the State Public Officials Act may not serve as a member of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee.
  (2) A person who falls under any of the subparagraphs of Article 31 of the Local Public Officials Act may not serve as a member of a regional mountainous district management committee.
  (3) The Administrator of the Korea Forest Service or the Mayor/Do Governor may dismiss a member of the Central Mountainous District Management Committee or a regional mountainous district management committee if the member falls under any of the following subparagraphs; provided, in the case of subparagraphs 1 or 2, the relevant member shall be dismissed: <Amended on Nov. 11, 2015>
  1. In case of committing an unfair act or abusing authority in relation to duties prescribed in Article 22 of the Act;
  2. In case of being unable to perform the duties of a committee member due to a disease, injury, etc.;
  3. In case of violating the fairness of the hearing or resolution, because the member fails to recuse oneself from participating in deliberation and resolution despite falling under any subparagraph of Article 29-3(1) or any subparagraph of Article 31-3(1);
  4. In cases where it is deemed unsuitable to maintain the position of a committee member due to neglect of duty, damage to dignity, or any other reasons;
  5. Where the member voluntarily admits that it is impracticable for him or her to perform his or her duties.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 24, 2008]

CHAPTER III COLLECTION OF EARTH AND STONE

SECTION 1 Collection of Earth and Stone

Article 32 (Procedures and examination of permission for extraction of earth and stones) (1) A person who intends to obtain permission to extract earth and stones or permission for modifications, or to notification of modifications under Article 25(1) of the Act shall submit an application to the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, along with documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 7, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu receives an application for permission to extract earth and stones or permission for modifications thereof, or receives a notification of modifications under paragraph (1), he or she shall conduct an on-site investigation into mountainous districts subject to permission for extracting earth and stones, permission for modifications, or notification of modifications, and shall examine whether the details of such application conform to the standards for permission to extract earth and stones as provided in Article 28 of the Act, and then undergo deliberation by a regional mountainous district management committee on the feasibility of extraction of earth or stone; provided, in any of the following cases, deliberation by a regional mountainous district management committee is not required: <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Jul. 24, 2008; Nov. 26, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; Dec. 31, 2014; Nov. 11, 2015; Dec. 30, 2016; Jun. 2, 2020; Dec. 16, 2021>
  1. In case of notification of modifications;
  2. In case of excavation or extraction of earth less than 50,000 square meters;
  3. In case of obtaining permission for extraction of earth and stones in order to excavate and extract earth and stones of less than 100,000 cubic meters produced incidentally in the process of converting or temporarily using a mountainous district (including cases of conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district after receiving administrative dispositions under which permission for conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district, or notification of conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district is deemed granted or excluded under other statutes and regulations; hereinafter the same shall apply);
  4. Where he or she intends to obtain permission to extract earth and stones to take out earth and stones already excavated, extracted, or brought in before the period of permission for extracting earth and stones expires;
  5. Where it is unavoidable to excavate or extract additional earth and stones in order to restore the slopes of an area where earth and stones have been extracted, and thus an earth and stones extraction permit is sought; provided, where the excavation or extraction is carried out by a person other than the State or a local government, it shall be limited to cases where the additionally excavated or extracted earth and stones are not transported outside the area subject to slope restoration;
  6. Where the modifications are intended to increase the volume of earth and stones to be extracted without changing the area of extraction;
  7. Where the installation or modification of ancillary facilities, such as a product processing site, access road, or other ancillary facilities prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, is intended without changing the volume of earth and stones to be extracted.
  (3) Where the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu deems it appropriate to grant permission for extracting earth and stones or permission for modifications, or to accept a notification of modifications as a result of examination under paragraph (2), they shall require the applicant to indicate the boundary of the area subject to permission, permission for modifications, or a notification of modifications, and a buffer zone under subparagraph 4 of Appendix 8, and make a restoration deposit under Article 38(1) of the Act in advance, and then issue a permit to extract earth and stones in the form prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs to the applicant or accept the notification of modifications. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; Nov. 26, 2009; Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) "Purposes prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 25(2) of the Act means cases where a person authorized to use or profit from mountainous districts or the owner of mountainous districts excavates or extracts earth and sand for self-consumption. <Added on Jul. 27, 2007>
  (5) "Scale prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 25(2) of the Act means a scale of not less than 30 cubic meters but not more than 1,000 cubic meters. <Added on Jul. 27, 2007>
  (6) The head of a related administrative agency who intends to request a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to hold consultation under Article 25(7) of the Act shall submit a written request for consultation on extraction of earth and stones, accompanied by documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. In such cases, paragraph (2) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the examination of requests for consultation on extraction of earth and stones. <Added on Aug. 22, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 2, 2020>
  (7) Deleted. <Dec. 7, 2010>
  [Title Amended on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 32-2 (Earth and stone extraction requiring permission and notification) "Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 25-2 of the Act means any of the following cases: <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Nov. 11, 2015; Dec. 30, 2016; Jun. 2, 2017; Mar. 3, 2020; Jun. 2, 2020; Nov. 24, 2020; Dec. 16, 2021; Jun. 14, 2022>
  1. Where a person specified in the following items (including a person who has been subject to an administrative disposition in which any of the following permissions or notifications are deemed granted under other statutes) excavates or extracts a rock in its original form with the longest straight length of 18 centimeters or more (hereinafter referred to as "natural stone") in the course of converting or temporarily using a mountainous district, and transports such natural stone outside the area of conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district (referring to the site specified in the relevant project plan, where a project plan has been formulated for the intended project of the conversion or temporary use of a mountainous district; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article):
  a. A person who has obtained permission to convert a mountainous district under Article 14 of the Act;
  b. A person who has filed a notification of the conversion of a mountainous district under Article 15 of the Act;
  c. A person who has obtained permission for temporary use of a mountainous district under Article 15-2(1) of the Act;
  d. A person who has filed a notification of temporary use of a mountainous district under Article 15-2(4) of the Act;
  2. Where a person falling under any of the items of subparagraph 1 (including a person who has been subject to an administrative disposition under which any of the following permissions or notifications are deemed granted or filed under other statutes) excavates or extracts earth and stones incidentally in the course of converting or temporarily using a mountainous district, and transports such earth and stones outside the area of conversion or temporary use of mountainous district, in a quantity of 50,000 cubic meters or more; provided, any of the following cases shall be excluded:
  a. In the case where the State, local governments, and institutions or organizations under Article 11(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Report on Real Estate Transactions convert or temporarily use a mountainous district to install the following facilities for official or public use:
  1) Facilities under subparagraph 3 of Article 10 of the Act;
  2) Facilities under Article 12(1)8 of the Act;
  3) Facilities under Article 12(2)5 of the Act;
  4) Facilities under subparagraph 1a through n of Appendix 5;
  b. Where a project implementer defined in subparagraph 8 of Article 2 of the Act on Public-Private Partnerships in Infrastructure converts or temporarily uses mountainous districts to install the following social infrastructure:
  1) Roads and road appurtenances under subparagraphs 1 and 2 of Article 2 of the Road Act;
  2) Railway under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Railroad Service Act;
  3) Urban railway under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Urban Railroad Act;
  4) Port facilities under subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Harbor Act;
  5) Airport facilities under subparagraph 7 of Article 2 of the Airport Facilities Act;
  6) Multi-purpose dams under the Act on Construction and Management of Dams and Assistance to Their Environs;
  7) Facilities necessary for education and training and barracks life as defined in subparagraph 1a of Article 2 of the Act on National Defense and Military Installations Projects, and facilities attached to military units as defined in item g of that subparagraph, which are necessary for the housing, welfare, and sports of soldiers;
  8) Public health care institutions under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of Public Health and Medical Services Act;
  9) Public buildings belonging to the central administrative agencies among the public office buildings under subparagraph 6d of Article 2 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  c. Deleted; <Nov. 24, 2020>
  d. Deleted; <Nov. 24, 2020>
  3. Where a person falling under subparagraph 2a or b of Article 25-2 of the Act incidentally excavates or extracts natural stone or bedrock (referring to a pure rock layer exposed as a rock vein without soil, in the underground portion designed for soil extraction) in the process of extracting earth and stones;
  4. In the case of excavating or extracting earth and stones containing minerals (including earth and stones extracted incidentally in the process of extracting minerals) in accordance with Article 27(2) of the Act for use or sale for non-mining purposes, such as construction, crafts, landscaping, crushed aggregate, or civil engineering purposes.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 7, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Nov. 24, 2020]
  [Previous Article 32-2 moved to Article 32-3 <Dec. 7, 2010>]

Article 32-3 (Restricted area for earth and stone extraction) (1) Mountainous districts where the excavation and extraction of earth and stones are restricted under Article 25-3(1)1 of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Nov. 30, 2007; Apr. 3, 2008; Jul. 24, 2008; Sep. 22, 2008; Apr. 20, 2009; Nov. 2, 2009; Nov. 26, 2009; Mar. 9, 2010; Dec. 7, 2010; Dec. 29, 2010; Jan. 28, 2014; Jul. 14, 2014; Nov. 11, 2015; Dec. 30, 2016; Jan. 16, 2018; Mar. 12, 2019; Jul. 2, 2019; May 26, 2020; May 7, 2024>
  1. A mountainous district within a 30-meter radius from the lower trunk of superior trees designated under Article 19(1) of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act and protected trees designated under Article 13(1) of the Forest Protection Act;
  2. A mountainous district within 100 meters from the boundary of any facilities corresponding to the following items:
  a. Railway and high-speed railway under subparagraphs 1 and 2 of Article 2 of the Act on Railroad Construction and Railroad Facilities Management;
  b. Orbit under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Tramway Transportation Act;
  c. A road that has begun to be used in whole or in part in accordance with Article 39 of the Road Act;
  d. Electric power source facilities defined in Article 2 of the Electric Power Source Development Promotion Act;
  e. Rivers under Article 7(1) of the River Act;
  f. Lakes and marshes under subparagraph 14 of Article 2 of the Water Environment Conservation Act;
  g. Reservoir under subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act;
  h. Ancestral shrines (jaegak);
  i. Natural recreation forests, forest bathing areas, and healing forests under subparagraphs 2, 3, and 5 of Article 2 of the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act;
  3. Deleted. <Nov. 26, 2009>
  4. Mountainous districts within 500 meters from the boundary of any facility or area specified in any of the following items:
  a. Military facilities under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Protection of Military Bases and Installations Act;
  b. Facilities of the central administrative agencies and special local administrative agencies under Articles 2 and 3 of the Government Organization Act, courts and registration offices under Article 3 of the Court Organization Act, and affiliated organizations of each relevant agency;
  c. Facilities of local governments under Articles 2 and 3 of the Local Autonomy Act, public corporations established under special laws, and affiliated institutions of each relevant organization;
  d. Schools defined in Article 2 of the Early Childhood Education Act, Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act, and Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
  e. Medical institutions under Article 3 of the Medical Service Act;
  f. Protection zones under the Act on the Conservation and Utilization of Modern and Contemporary Cultural Heritage or the Act on Conservation and Utilization of Natural Heritage (in the case of cultural heritage or natural heritage for which a protection zone has not been designated, referring to the cultural heritage or natural heritage).
  (2) Mountainous districts, where the excavation or extraction of earth or stones is restricted under Article 25-3(1)2 of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010; Dec. 31, 2014>
  1. Mountainous districts within 2,000 meters of visually exposed areas along expressways and railways;
  2. Mountainous districts within 1,000 meters of visually exposed areas along national highways;
  3. Mountainous districts within 500 meters of visually exposed areas along local roads; provided, this shall not apply where a person who obtained a quarrying permit for such mountainous district within 500 meters of a visually exposed area along local road before May 16, 2000, continues to quarry or extract earth and sand in an area adjoining the permitted site;
  4. Mountainous district located in the waterfront visibility area of a port district as defined in subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Port Act, and Mountainous district within 500 meters of the high-tide shoreline.
  (3) In Article 25-3(1)4 of the Act, "mountainous district in areas prescribed by Presidential Decree" means following mountainous districts: <Amended on Mar. 9, 2010; Dec. 7, 2010; Dec. 29, 2010; Jul. 31, 2012; Jul. 20, 2015; Nov. 11, 2015; May 26, 2020; May 7, 2024>
  1. Mountainous districts within arboretums and gardens defined in subparagraphs 1 and 1-2 of Article 2 of the Act on the Creation and Furtherance of Arboretums and Gardens;
  2. Mountainous districts in land for erosion control under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Erosion Control Work Act;
  3. Mountainous districts within special wildlife protection zones under Article 27 of the Wildlife Protection and Management Act;
  4. Mountainous districts within a protection zone (in the case of cultural or natural heritage for which a protection zone has not been designated) according to the Act on the Conservation and Utilization of Modern and Contemporary Cultural Heritage or the Act on Conservation and Utilization of Natural Heritage;
  5. Seed stand under Article 19(1) of the Act on the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act, and experiment forest under Article 47(1) of that Act, and forest protection zone under Article 7(1) of the Forest Protection Act;
  6. Mountainous districts in natural recreation forests under Article 13 of the Forestry Culture and Recreation Act, and mountainous districts in forest bathing areas and healing forests created under Article 20 of that Act.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2007]
  [Moved from Article 32-2, previous Article 32-3 moved to Article 32-4 <Dec. 7, 2010>]

Article 32-4 (Exceptions to restrictions on activities in restricted areas for earth and stone extraction) (1) The term "project prescribed by Presidential Decree in subparagraph 2 of Article 25-4 of the Act" means a project for installing roads, railroads, tramways, canals, or waterways. <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010>
  (2) In subparagraph 3 of Article 25-4 of the Act, "where necessary for an official or public project, as prescribed by Presidential Decree" means any of the following cases: <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008; Apr. 20, 2009; Nov. 26, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; May 22, 2012; Dec. 31, 2014; Dec. 30, 2016; Jun. 2, 2020; Nov. 24, 2020; Aug. 23, 2022>
  1. Where the head of a relevant central administrative agency (including the head of a special local administrative agency under Article 3 of the Government Organization Act, and referring to the head of the relevant local government in cases of construction projects for nationally supported local roads) makes a request for the use of earth and stones for a project specified in any subparagraph of Article 4 of the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects, specifying the person who extracts the earth and stones , the location and area of the extraction site, the type and quantity of the earth and stones to be extracted, and the extraction period, and such request is deemed appropriate;
  2. Where a person intends to obtain permission for the extraction of earth and stones incidentally produced in the course of converting or temporarily using mountainous districts;
  3. In case of installing auxiliary facilities necessary for earth and stone extraction (limited to access roads or management offices) (only applicable to mountainous districts under Article 32-3(1)2 and paragraph (2)1 through 3);
  4. In the case of attempting to excavate or extract new earth and stones adjacent to a permitted area where excavation and extraction of earth and stones is in progress with a permit for extraction of earth and stones, which fall under any of the following items (only applicable to mountainous districts under Article 32-3(2)1 through 3):
  a. Where a local road is reclassified as a general national road or an expressway;
  b. Where a general national road is reclassified as an expressway;
  c. Where a Si/Gun road is reclassified as a local road;
  d. Where the use of all or part of the section of a national expressway, general national highway, or local highway is commenced under Article 39 of the Road Act;
  e. In cases where a public notice of completion of a railway is issued under Article 16(2) of the Act on Railroad Construction and Railroad Facilities Management, or a pre-completion use permission is granted under the proviso of paragraph (4) of that Article;
  5. Where a person intends to excavate or extract earth and stones from a mountainous district adjacent to an area for which permission for extraction of earth and stones has been granted (including an area under restoration or already restored after the expiration of the permission period), and where the continued extraction of the remaining mountainous district, the area of which is less than 50,000 square meters, would result in the formation of flat land without any slopes (limited to mountainous districts specified in subparagraphs 1 through 3 of Article 32-3(2));
  6. In any of the following cases:
  a. Where a person seeks to excavate or extract underground stone (limited to stone) in an area for which an earth and stones extraction permit has been granted (including a site currently under restoration or already restored after permit expiration);
  b. Where it is intended to take out the rocks already excavated or extracted after expiration of the permit to extract earth and stones (limited to rocks);
  c. Where it is unavoidable to additionally excavate or extract earth and stones for the purpose of restoring the slope of an earth and stones extraction site (limited to slopes adjacent to extraction-restricted areas); provided, where an entity other than the State or a local government excavates or extracts such earth and stones, this shall be limited to cases where the additionally excavated or extracted earth and stones are not transported outside the area subject to slope restoration;
  7. In cases of excavating or extracting earth and stones in an area designated as a quarry complex under Article 29 of the Act.
  (3) In subparagraph 4 of Article 25-4 of the Act, "cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as where consent is obtained from the manager or owner of the relevant public facilities, etc." means any of the following cases: <Amended on Apr. 20, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; Dec. 30, 2016; May 7, 2024>
  1. In the case of a region or facility specified in Article 32-3(1)4 and (3) 4, where consent has been obtained from the management office or custodian of the region or facility (in the case of a protection zone under the Act on the Conservation and Utilization of Modern and Contemporary Cultural Heritage or a protection zone under the Act on Conservation and Utilization of Natural Heritage, referring to the Administrator of the Korea Heritage Service; in the case of a military facility, referring to the Minister of National Defense or the commander of the relevant unit);
  2. In the case of coastal areas located 100 meters away from dedicated railways not intended for passenger transport (only applicable to the mountainous district under Article 32-3(2)1);
  3. In the case of a mountainous district within 100 meters from the boundary of a ritual shrine (jegak), and consent has been obtained from the manager or owner of the shrine.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2007]
  [Title Amended on May 22, 2012]
  [Moved from Article 32-3 <Dec. 7, 2010>]

Article 33 Deleted. <Jul. 27, 2007>

Article 34 (Evaluation of economic feasibility of quarrying) (1) In the main clause of Article 26(1) of the Act, "specialized research institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" means an institution that falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Jan. 9, 2004; Aug. 5, 2005; Feb. 29, 2008; Apr. 20, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 11, 2015; Jul. 9, 2019; Aug. 31, 2021; Mar. 11, 2025; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. National Institute of Forest Science;
  2. Korea Mine Rehabilitation and Mineral Resources Corporation under the Korea Mine Rehabilitation and Mineral Resources Corporation Act;
  3. An engineering business entity that has filed a notification with the Minister of Trade, Industry and Energy in accordance with Article 21(1) of the Engineering Industry Promotion Act to conduct engineering activities in the mining or construction sector (limited to specialized fields of soil and geology);
  4. A corporation that conducts geological surveys and mineral resource research projects among the research institutes listed in each subparagraph of Appendix of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Science and Technology Research Institutes;
  5. A technical engineer's office in the mining or construction sector (limited to the soil and geology field) established and registered by a professional engineer in accordance with Article 6 of the Professional Engineers Act;
  6. Korea Forest Conservation Association;
  7. Korea Quarrying Association under Article 36-2 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Korea Quarrying Association");
  8. Korea Forestry Promotion Institute;
  9. Other institutions or organizations recognized by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service as having the capacity to conduct quarrying economic feasibility assessment.
  (2) In the proviso of Article 26(1) of the Act, "cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" means any of the following cases: <Amended on Aug. 5, 2005; Jul. 27, 2007; Dec. 7, 2010; May 22, 2012; Aug. 22, 2012; Nov. 11, 2015; Dec. 16, 2021>
  1. In cases of excavating or extracting rocks for civil engineering or landscaping purposes;
  2. In cases of excavating or extracting underground stone beneath the area for which an earth and stones extraction permit has been granted and in which rocks are excavated or extracted;
  3. In cases of excavating or extracting rocks incidentally extracted in the process of converting or temporarily using mountainous districts, or natural stones referred to in the latter part of Article 28(3) of the Act;
  4. In cases of taking out rocks that are already excavated, extracted, or brought in before the expiration of the stone quarrying permission period, after the period has expired;
  5. Where it is unavoidable to excavate or extract rocks for the purpose of restoring the slope in the area where rocks are excavated or extracted;
  6. In cases of expanding the area of earth and stones extraction site for which an earth and stone extraction permit has been granted by 20/100 (only applicable to the cases where the bedrock is exposed and the type, quality, etc. of the rocks are deemed identical to that of the mountainous districts for which the initial permit for extracting earth and stones was granted).
  (3) The methods and standards for evaluating the economic feasibility of quarrying under Article 26(2) of the Act are as specified in Appendix 7.

Article 35 (Extraction of earth and stones within mining claim) In the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 27(1) of the Act, "any specialized research institution prescribed by Presidential Decree" means an agency provided in Article 34(1)2 through 7. <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010; Jul. 9, 2019>
  [Title Amended on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 36 (Criteria for permission for extraction of earth and stones) (1) In Article 28(1)2 of the Act, "criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the criteria set forth in Appendix 8.
  (2) In Article 28(1)4 of the Act, "the areas prescribed by Presidential Decree" means any of the following mountainous districts where excavating or extracting earth and stones directly or indirectly affect the living environment, etc.: <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Nov. 11, 2015>
  1. Mountainous districts within 300 meters from houses, livestock facilities, factories, or religious facilities;
  2. Mountainous districts within 30 meters of the center point of a burial site.
  (3) In Article 28(1)4 of the Act, "standards prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the installation of facilities to prevent disasters" means each of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 24, 2008; Apr. 20, 2009; Nov. 26, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; Jul. 20, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 11, 2015; Jul. 2, 2019>
  1. The area shall not be one determined to have a high risk of landslide, based on risk factors such as slope gradient, parent rock, and forest conditions, under the landslide risk assessment criteria table prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, nor an area where a landslide has occurred; provided, this shall not apply where permission is granted on the condition that disaster prevention facilities are installed;
  2. If a disaster is likely to occur in neighboring areas, the following protective measures shall be reflected in the project plan:
  a. Installation of drainage facilities and other structures to prevent soil erosion and slope collapse on cut-and-fill surfaces (referring to surfaces formed by cutting or embankment);
  b. Installation of rockfall prevention facilities;
  c. Adoption of protective methods for slope stability;
  d. Installation of wind-blown dust control facilities, such as dust-proof nets;
  e. Adoption of low-noise and low-vibration blasting methods;
  f. Measures to dispose of topsoil and waste stones;
  3. Consent shall be obtained for each of the following purposes; provided, this shall not apply to cases where an environmental impact assessment or small-scale environmental impact assessment has been conducted in accordance with the Environmental Impact Assessment Act:
  a. In the case specified in paragraph (2)1, the consent of all of the following persons is required: The owner of the relevant house or livestock facility, the residents (meaning the head of a household who actually resides there under the Resident Registration Act), the owner and the representative of the factory, and all representatives of religious facilities (if the extraction permit is sought for an area adjacent to a mountainous district where earth and stones are excavated or extracted with earth and stones extraction permit, the consent of at least two-thirds of the representatives of the religious facilities shall suffice);
  b. In the case of paragraph (2)2, the consent shall be obtained from a relative defined in subparagraph 16 of Article 2 of the Act on Funeral Services (limited to where a relative exists);
  (4) The standards for stone excavation and extraction equipment and technical personnel that shall be met by an applicant for a stone extraction permit (limited to rocks) under the main clause of Article 28(1)5 of the Act shall be as specified in Appendix 8-2. <Amended on Oct. 30, 2018>
  (5) "Distance prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 28(1)6 of the Act means the distance categorized in the following: <Added on Jun. 2, 2020>
  1. Earth and stones for the restoration of a completed extraction site: No distance limit applies;
  2. Earth and stones specified in subparagraph 1 of Article 25-2 of the Act: Within a straight-line distance of 60 kilometers from the boundary of the area for which an earth and stones extraction permit is sought.
  (6) "Scale prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 28(1)6 of the Act means the scale according to the following categories: <Added on Jun. 2, 2020>
  1. Earth and stones for the restoration of a completed extraction site: Amount of earth and stone recorded in the design plan for restoration of mountainous districts approved under Article 40(1) of the Act;
  2. Earth and stones specified in subparagraph 1 of Article 25-2 of the Act: 50/100 of the amount of earth and stones to be extracted for which a permit for earth and stones extraction is sought under Article 25(1) of the Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 37 (Exceptions to application of criteria for permission for extraction of earth and stones) (1) In Article 28(2)2 of the Act, "projects prescribed by Presidential Decree" means projects for installing roads, railroads, tramways, canals, or waterways. <Amended on Dec. 2010>
  (2) In Article 28(2)3 of the Act, "cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" means any of the following cases: <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008; Nov. 26, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; Nov. 11, 2015; Dec. 16, 2021>
  1. Where the head of a relevant central administrative agency (including the head of a special local administrative agency under Article 3 of the Government Organization Act, and referring to the head of the relevant local government in the case of projects involving nationally supported local roads) makes a request for the use of earth and stones for a project specified in any subparagraph of Article 4 of the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects, specifying the earth and stones extractor, the location and area of the extraction site, the type, quantity, and extraction period of the earth and stones, and such request is deemed appropriate;
  2. Where a person intends to obtain permission for the extraction of earth and stones incidentally produced in the course of converting or temporarily using mountainous districts;
  3. In case of attempting to take out earth and stones already excavated, extracted, or brought in before the permits for the earth and stones extraction expires after the permission period has expired;
  4. In cases where it is unavoidable to excavate or extract earth and stones for the purpose of restoring the slope of an earth and stones extraction site (limited to cases where the excavated or extracted earth and stones are not transported out of the relevant earth and stones extraction site).
  (3) In accordance with Article 28(2) of the Act, all or some of the criteria for permission for extraction of earth and stones may not be applied in any of the following cases: <Amended on Jul. 24, 2008; Nov. 11, 2015; Jun. 2, 2020; Dec. 16, 2021>
  1. Cases where the standards under Article 36 are not applied:
  a. In cases of Article 28(2)1 of the Act;
  b. In cases where earth and stones are excavated and extracted incidentally in the process of excavating a tunnel or drift for the project specified in paragraph (1) and used for the project;
  c. In cases of paragraph (2)1 or 3;
  2. In cases where the criteria specified in Article 36(1) are not applied:
  a. In cases of paragraph (2)2;
  b. In cases of paragraph (2)4;
  3. In cases where Article 36(3)3a is not applied. In such cases, cases specified in item a shall be subject to deliberation by a regional mountainous district management committee:
  a. In case specified in paragraph (2)2 (limited to urban areas under Article 36 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act);
  b. In cases of paragraph (2)4;
  4. Cases where Article 36(4) is not applied:
  a. In cases specified in paragraph (2)2; provided, this shall not apply in cases where the extracted earth and stones are to be directly processed;
  b. In cases of paragraph (2)4;
  5. Cases where Article 28(1)6 of the Act is not applied: Where it falls under Article 28(2)1 of the Act;
  6. Notwithstanding the proviso of Article 28(1)6 of the Act, cases where earth and stones specified in subparagraph 1 of Article 25-2 of the Act may not be brought in: Cases falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (2).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jul. 27, 2007]

Article 38 (Size of natural stones) (1) In the former part, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 28(3) of the Act, "rocks of a size greater than that prescribed by Presidential Decree" means natural rocks under subparagraph 1 of Article 32-2. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2007; Nov. 26, 2009; Dec. 7, 2010; Aug. 22, 2012>
  (2) Deleted. <Aug. 22, 2012>

Article 39 (Designation of quarry complex) (1) In accordance with Article 29(1) of the Act, the following persons may designate or change the designation of quarry complexes according to their classification, either ex officio or upon application. <Added on Sep. 24, 2014>  (1) In accordance with Article 29(1) of the Act, the following persons may designate or change the designation of quarry complexes according to their classification, either ex officio or upon application: <Added on Sep. 24, 2014>
  2. Mayor/Do governor: Quarry complex, the area of which is at least 200,000 square meters but less than 300,000 square meters.
